.TH CELL 2
.SH NAME
cellopen, cellclose, cellsize, cellpixsize, cellresize, cellscroll, cellinvalidate, cellstyle, cellput, cellputs, cellwidth, cellpoint, cellfill, cellclear, cellloadthemestr, cellloadtheme, cellsetstyle, cellmix, cellflush \- cell grid of styled text drawn with libdraw
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
#include <u.h>
#include <libc.h>
#include <draw.h>
#include <cell.h>
.sp 0.3v
typedef struct Cell Cell;
.sp 0.3v
Cell*	cellopen(Font*)
void	cellclose(Cell*)
void	cellsize(Cell*, int *rows, int *cols)
void	cellpixsize(Cell*, int *w, int *h)
int	cellresize(Cell*)
void	cellscroll(Cell*, int row, int nrows, int n)
void	cellinvalidate(Cell*)
int	cellstyle(Cell*, char *name)
int	cellput(Cell*, int row, int col, Rune, int style)
int	cellputs(Cell*, int row, int col, char *utf8, int style)
int	cellwidth(Cell*, Rune)
int	cellpoint(Cell*, Point, int *row, int *col)
void	cellfill(Cell*, int row, int col, int nrows, int ncols,
		Rune, int style)
void	cellclear(Cell*)
int	cellloadthemestr(Cell*, char *text)
int	cellloadtheme(Cell*, char *path)
int	cellsetstyle(Cell*, char *name, ulong rgbfg, ulong rgbbg,
		int attr)
int	cellmix(Cell*, int a, int b)
int	cellflush(Cell*)
.sp 0.3v
enum { Cdefault = 0 };
enum { Cunderline = 1, Creverse = 2 };
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Libcell
keeps a grid of
.I rows
by
.I cols
character cells, each holding a rune and a style, and draws it into the
current window of a
.IR libdraw (2)
program.
Only the cells that changed since the last flush are redrawn.
It uses no terminal and no escape sequences.
It starts no processes, reads no mouse or keyboard, and uses no global state;
the caller owns the event loop.
.PP
.I Cellopen
creates a grid that fills
.BR screen ,
using the given
.IR Font ,
which the caller keeps and must keep valid until
.IR cellclose .
It measures the width of an ASCII character; that width and
.B font->height
are the size of every cell.
A proportional font is refused:
.I cellopen
returns nil and the error string names the differing widths.
.I Cellclose
frees the grid and every image it allocated.
.SS Sizes
.I Cellsize
stores the number of rows and columns in
.I *rows
and
.IR *cols .
They are 0 when the window is smaller than one cell.
.I Cellpixsize
stores the pixel width and height of one cell.
Rows and columns are numbered from 0 at the top left.
.SS Styles and themes
A style is a small integer id naming a foreground colour, a background colour
and the attributes underline and reverse.
Ids are passed to the functions that put text.
.PP
The built-in theme defines seven styles, with ids 0 to 6:
.BR default ,
.BR keyword ,
.BR comment ,
.BR string ,
.BR error ,
.BR selection
and
.BR cursor .
.B Default
has id
.BR Cdefault .
.I Cellstyle
returns the id of the named style, or
.B Cdefault
if there is none.
.PP
.I Cellsetstyle
defines a style at run time with colours
.B 0xRRGGBB
and
.I attr
made of
.B Cunderline
and
.BR Creverse ,
and returns its id.
Redefining an existing name changes it in place and keeps its id.
It returns \-1 and sets the error string if the table is full or an argument is bad.
.PP
.I Cellmix
returns the id of a style made from
.I a
with every field that
.I b
sets overriding it.
Asking again for the same pair gives the same id (the result is cached).
If
.I b
is
.B Cdefault
or
.I a
equals
.IR b ,
the result is
.IR a .
It returns \-1 and sets the error string for a bad id or when the table is full.
.PP
.I Cellloadthemestr
and
.I cellloadtheme
read a theme from a string or from a file (at most 64 kilobytes).
Entries are merged by name over the current styles, built-in ones first,
so a theme that names only
.B keyword
changes only that style.
A named style is replaced wholesale.
Each line has the form
.IP
.EX
name key=value ... flag ...
.EE
.PP
where
.I name
is the style name, the keys are
.B fg=#rrggbb
and
.B bg=#rrggbb
(six hex digits), and the flags are
.B underline
and
.BR reverse .
Fields are separated by blanks.
Lines that are empty or begin with
.B #
are comments.
A colour left unset follows the
.B default
style; no attribute is set except those listed.
For example:
.IP
.EX
# comment
default   fg=#e0e0e0 bg=#101040
keyword   fg=#ffaf00
error     fg=#ff0000 underline
.EE
.PP
The load is all or nothing.
On a bad line the functions return \-1, change nothing, and set the error
string to
.BI theme: LINE : " message"
(a file that cannot be read or is too large gives the usual error instead).
They return 0 on success.
.SS Putting text
.I Cellput
stores one rune at a cell and returns the number of cells stored:
0 if the position is outside the grid, 1 or 2 otherwise.
.I Cellputs
stores a UTF-8 string from the given position, without wrapping;
text past the right edge is clipped.
It returns the number of cells stored.
.I Cellfill
stores the same rune and style in the rectangle of
.I nrows
by
.I ncols
cells, clipped to the grid.
.I Cellclear
sets every cell to a space in the
.B default
style.
Nothing is drawn until
.IR cellflush .
.PP
Width rules.
Each rune is stored as a span of whole cells.
The span is the measured width of the rune in the font in pixels divided by the
cell width, rounded up; it is measured once per rune and remembered.
Control characters and rune 0 are stored as a space.
A rune with no glyph width, or wider than two cells, is stored as U+FFFD.
A wide rune occupies two cells; overwriting either half of it turns
the other half into a space.
A wide rune that does not fit at the last column is stored as a space.
If a glyph is narrower than its cells, the rest is painted with the background.
.PP
.I Cellwidth
returns the number of cells
.I cellput
would store for the rune: 1 or 2, by the rules above, so a caller can lay out
text exactly as it will be drawn.
Only the clip cases differ: outside the grid nothing is stored, and a wide
rune at the last column is stored as one space.
It returns 0 for a nil
.IR Cell .
.SS Drawing
.I Cellflush
compares the grid with what it drew last and redraws only the cells that
differ, as runs of equal style, one drawing request per run, and then calls
.I flushimage
once.
It returns the number of cells redrawn (a wide rune counts 2), or \-1.
The margins left over when the window is not a whole number of cells are
painted with the background of
.BR default .
.SS Window changes
After every resize or move of the window the caller must call
.IR getwindow (2)
and then
.IR cellresize ,
and then refill the grid as needed and call
.IR cellflush .
.I Cellresize
re-reads the window rectangle and resizes the grid;
the next flush redraws everything.
It returns 0, or \-1 and sets the error string if the window is gone
(the grid is then kept as it was, and drawing does nothing).
A window smaller than one cell gives a grid of 0 rows or 0 columns,
on which all functions are harmless; a later resize brings the grid back.
.PP
.I Cellinvalidate
makes the next
.I cellflush
redraw every cell and the margins, for example after something else drew on the window.
.SS Mouse
.I Cellpoint
maps a point in screen coordinates, as delivered in
.B Mouse.xy
(see
.IR mouse (2)),
to the cell under it, stored in
.I *row
and
.IR *col .
It returns 1 if the point is inside the grid and 0 if it is in the margin
or outside the window; in that case
.I *row
and
.I *col
are clamped to the nearest cell.
A point in the right half of a wide rune gives that cell's own column;
the caller maps it to the rune.
With no window
.RI ( screen
is nil) or an empty grid it sets both to 0 and returns 0.
.SS Scrolling
.I Cellscroll
moves the
.I nrows
rows starting at
.I row
by
.I n
rows, up if
.I n
is positive and down if negative, in the grid and on the screen with a single
drawing request.
The rows uncovered by the move become spaces in the
.B default
style; the caller puts the new text there before the next flush.
Rows moved by scrolling are not redrawn by
.IR cellflush .
.SH EXAMPLES
Open the grid, then redraw after a resize:
.IP
.EX
void
eresized(int new)
{
	if(new && getwindow(display, Refnone) < 0)
		sysfatal("getwindow: %r");
	if(cellresize(cell) < 0)
		fprint(2, "cellresize: %r\en");
	redraw();	/* cellput and cellputs calls */
	if(cellflush(cell) < 0)
		fprint(2, "cellflush: %r\en");
}

void
main(void)
{
	Font *f;
	int kw;

	initdraw(nil, nil, "demo");
	f = openfont(display, "/lib/font/bit/vga/unicode.font");
	cell = cellopen(f);
	if(cell == nil)
		sysfatal("cellopen: %r");
	kw = cellstyle(cell, "keyword");
	cellputs(cell, 0, 0, "hello, 世界", kw);
	cellflush(cell);
	/* event loop calling eresized(1) on resize */
}
.EE
.PP
Scroll the text of a window up one line and fill the new last line:
.IP
.EX
cellsize(cell, &rows, &cols);
cellscroll(cell, 0, rows, 1);
cellputs(cell, rows-1, 0, newline, Cdefault);
cellflush(cell);
.EE
.SH SOURCE
The libcell repository (not part of
.BR /sys/src ):
.B cell.h
and the C files beside it, built with
.IR mk (1).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR graphics (2),
.IR draw (2),
.IR allocimage (2),
.IR font (6)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Functions that can fail return \-1 or nil and set the error string, readable
with
.BR %r .
.I Libcell
never calls
.IR sysfatal .
A position outside the grid is not an error: such calls store nothing.
.SH BUGS
Fixed-width fonts only.
There is no bold or italic, and combining marks are not supported: each rune
takes its own cell or cells.
Runes the font does not map are drawn as the font's missing-glyph box
(rune 0 of the font).
A wide rune whose glyph is not exactly its cells wide is padded with the background.
